// Undiscounted: [tosink] never reaches the target, so it's worth Infinity -
// worst case for min, best case for max.

// RESULT: 10.0
Rmin=?[F s=3]

// RESULT: Infinity
Rmax=?[F s=3]

// With a discount factor (discount=0.9, via the F operator's
// {discount=...} option), [tosink]'s value is now merely 1 + 0.9*0 = 1
// (s=4 has no reward, so its discounted value is 0) rather than Infinity,
// so the minimiser now *prefers* [tosink] (1) over the guaranteed-reaching
// [totarget] (10) - the opposite of the undiscounted case. The maximiser
// still prefers [totarget] (10 > 1).

// RESULT: 1.0
Rmin=?[F{discount=0.9} s=3]

// RESULT: 10.0
Rmax=?[F{discount=0.9} s=3]

// Discounting is only implemented for a simple reachability target (F "target"), not for
// general co-safe LTL. The grammar lets {discount=...} be attached to the outermost F of
// any co-safe formula, so that has to be reported as unsupported rather than silently
// computing the undiscounted value (which here would be Infinity, as above).
//
// These two report as UNSUPPORTED rather than PASS: the test framework treats a
// PrismNotSupportedException as "skip this configuration" before it ever looks at the
// RESULT below (see ResultTesting.checkExceptionAgainstExpectedResultString). They are
// still worth having - if the discount is ever silently ignored again, a number comes
// back instead of an exception and the RESULT below turns the regression into a FAIL.

// RESULT: Error:co-safe
Rmax=?[F{discount=0.9} X (s=3)]

// RESULT: Error:co-safe
Rmin=?[F{discount=0.9} (s=0 U s=3)]
